findings.recommendation:
Always increment both counters on any failed login, including non-existent email. Move INCR (and EXPIRE-on-first) into a shared failure path before throwing 401:

private Mono<User> handleUserNotFound(String password, String emailKey, String ipKey) {
    return Mono.fromCallable(() -> passwordEncoder.matches(password, DUMMY_HASH))
            .subscribeOn(Schedulers.boundedElastic())
            .then(registerFailure(emailKey, ipKey))
            .then(Mono.error(AppException.unauthorized("Invalid credentials")));
}

This also closes a DoS hole: without per-email increment for unknown emails, an attacker could spray through a 5-email rotation forever from one IP without ever tripping the per-email key.

Canonicalize the email at the controller/service entry before any DB or Redis use:

String email = request.getEmail().trim().toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT);

Apply the same normalization in registration, password-reset, forgot-password (Tasks 5/6). Add a unit test that login with 'USER@TEST.COM' shares the same Redis counter as 'user@test.com'.

Gate header trust on a config flag and document the trust requirement:

@Value("${app.security.trust-forwarded-headers:false}") boolean trustXff;

private String extractIp(ServerWebExchange exchange) {
    if (trustXff) {
        String xff = exchange.getRequest().getHeaders().getFirst("X-Forwarded-For");
        if (xff != null && !xff.isBlank()) {
            int comma = xff.indexOf(',');
            return (comma > 0 ? xff.substring(0, comma) : xff).trim();
        }
    }
    var remote = exchange.getRequest().getRemoteAddress();
    return remote != null && remote.getAddress() != null ? remote.getAddress().getHostAddress() : "unknown";
}

In production deploy, set TRUST_FORWARDED_HEADERS=true only when an ingress strips/sets the header. Add Spring's ForwardedHeaderTransformer with a configurable trusted-IP-range filter is an even stronger pattern. Add an integration test that without the flag, a forged header is ignored.
